now pretty patel came into her job as  home secretary apologizing for what she  herself called the terrible injustices  of the windrush scandal  but many of the families torn apart part  by the wrongful deportation of people to  the caribbean say they've still not been  compensated for the hurt they were  caused  i'm now joined by a member of the  independent advisory group who delivered  the windrush lessons learned review the  human rights lawyer jacqueline mckenzie  good morning miss mckenzie  i want to come to the crisis in the  channel in a moment but let's  start about uh talking by talking about  the windrush scheme many of those  entitled to compensation from the home  office uh say they've had little or  nothing out of it um  do you know how many were entitled how  many have claimed and  who's got how many have got money out of  this scheme  yes um i i wouldn't say that absolutely  no one has had anything out of it um  but you raise a really important point  in terms of what are the figures that  we're looking at because i don't think  it's very clear the home office has said  that 15 000 people should have availed  themselves of compensation  or being able to make a claim  at the outset though when the scandal  first came to i suppose into the public  domain the figure that we were given was  about 50 000 people 50 000 people  that would avail themselves services of  the windrush task force i don't know  where the 15 000 has come from that's  the starting point  but even if we go  on the basis of the 15 000  i understand just on the 3 000 has made  a claim and just over 800 have had any  payment  the home office say that one of the  reasons for that is that they're not  completely resourced and i say well if  you're not completely resourced for so  few applications  what would have happened  if you'd had anything near what you were  actually expecting  in terms of the payments there's lots of  problems about that  people are very unhappy with some of the  offers particularly for loss of earnings  lost pension  there have been some improvements the  impact of life tariff we think is an  area that has vastly improved there are  preliminary payments which was  introduced by the home secretary in  december 2020  there's still problems with that in that  very few people are actually getting it  but on paper i consider that to be an  improvement  but overall  the processing of the scheme seems  woefully inadequate at the moment  you have a number of clients who are in  this position who haven't got money or  it's been very difficult  tell us what impact this is making on  their lives  the impact is huge because you know  remember that this at the start of this  we found that we've lost jobs people had  lost houses some were detained removed  deported  and if you're now four years after the  scandal  and i know the scheme's only been in  operational compensation scheme that it  is for two and a half years but if  you've been you know traumatized by  being told you're an illegal immigrants  or an illegal migrants in this country  for over four years some for much longer  than that  and you're now having to wait it's an  ordinary time to either have a primary  offer to have your claim accepted or to  be paid out  given that you've already come from a a  place of deprivation remember the cohort  within the windrush generation who are  affected by the scandal are mainly very  deprived and challenged people who have  experienced lower socioeconomic outcomes  um and and and so  this is  unconscionable that they are having you  know to wait so long  what would you like the home secretary  to do about it now  well i mean i gave evidence to the home  affairs committee and one of their  recommendations is that the scheme  should come away from the home office  and go to an independent body  i've now come around i was concerned  myself about delays that that would  pause given how long people have been  waiting at the outset i was one of the  people that gave evidence during the  consultation exercise that said the home  office shouldn't have had the steam in  the first place i mean in effect  they are the people that have carried  out the wrongs i mean it is effectively  they're marking their own homework so  that's pretty um disconcerting to me but  i thought well they've got it where  where where we at um they've made some  improvements but i think  where we are today  the fact that we are seeing so many  people still struggling to even get  through this system the fact that we're  still seeing people in the home office  requesting copious amounts of evidence  it's not necessary asking ridiculous  questions of claimants the fact and the  home affairs committee found this too  that there's considerable mistrust  amongst the community affected with the  home office you know at the same time  they're seeing the deportations seeing  the discussions about refugees and so  forth i think the time has possibly come  now to take the scheme away from him  from the home office who it would go to  is it's a different question  but it would be an independent body you  think i i think it should be an  independent body um one of the problems  at the moment is that the windross  scandal has actually been guarded by  very tiny grassroots organizations  across the country who are helping  people to get status documents to make  exceptional payment claims or to claim  compensation  it isn't the large ngo and there's no  funding for this work and i think you  know  because of that because a very deprived  group of people are finding it difficult  to access the scheme properly it  probably now needs to go to either a  very big ngo or professional body  all right um  you have clients i think who have  taken the route that we've seen a lot of  news about this week across  the channel um  the government wants to make the manner  in which asylum seekers arrive in  britain um part of the judgment uh about  their claim uh for asylum  um they think that that would deter some  people from taking this very dangerous  route do you agree  no i don't agree and i think the  governments are all out of order to even  consider that because people are  entitled to get to a place of safety and  i know it's predicated on the fact that  you know a lot of them are coming from  france  even though you know they've passed  through lots of other countries but i  suppose this current debate is about  people coming from france and making  that perilous 25-mile journey across the  the channel um and it's predicated on  the fact that france is very safe  country  but we do not know what the experiences  are in france in fact just this weekend  i've had friends french lawyers  from paris and colleagues from london  who went to calais to distribute food  aid parcels and we're told it's now  illegal to do that  we have the question of muslim women not  being able to wear the the the knick out  so you know is france a safe country for  everybody it might not be and they are  all we've already rehearsed the reasons  why people choose to come to the uk  because colonial dies language ties  family the reception  but to be clear jacqueline kennedy  you're not really putting  the uh wearing or not wearing of nikub  um  as a reason for people to  risk this  crossing i mean france is  a democratic  law uh  rule of law  country  explain why it is  that people have to risk this crossing  from a country that is just as safe as  britain is yes so i'm certainly not  putting the wearing off the cab as an  explanation  that would probably be relevant to a  very small group of people but you know  we always try to say that countries are  all alike and they're not people have  different experiences people have  different reasons for wanting to move on  to yet another country and they are  entitled to do that that has all sorts  of problems and we are looking at this  in a very basic way um you know what  they come here and so on and there are  all sorts of qualifications i accept you  know are the crossings themselves legal  without authorization if you enter a  report without authorization but article  31 of the refugee convention actually  gives people protection from entering  countries without documentation and  without the rights to do so and and so  people are entitled to make those  crossings now they're making an  extremely perilous journey  we know some of it not all of it but  some of it is fueled by criminal gangs  but you know there aren't any other ways  for a lot of people to do that we know  that the dutch amendment scrapped we  know about problems with dublin because  we've come out because of brexit and one  of the uh this and andy spoke earlier  about the afghan citizens resettlement  scheme when is that going to start we  are getting daily calls from afghans who  want to be able to find a safe route to  claim asylum and they're entitled to do  so  jacqueline mckenzie um it's it's been  good to talk to you thank you very much  indeed thank you
 

